<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<preface xml:id="preface-introduction" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction</title>

  <section xml:id="sec-welcome">
    <title>Welcome</title>
    <p>
      This book is written for you — a Junior Cycle mathematics student in Ireland. It
      brings the NCCA curriculum to life in an interactive, online format. You'll work
      through five mathematical strands: <term>Geometry</term>, <term>Number</term>,
      <term>Algebra</term>, and <term>Statistics</term>. Throughout, you'll apply
      cross-cutting skills in the <term>Unifying Strand</term> — problem-solving,
      reasoning, representation, and communication that tie everything together.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-udl-aal">
    <title>How This Book Works: Universal Design for Learning (UDL) and Assessment as Learning (AAL)</title>
    <p>
      This book is designed so that every student can learn in ways that suit them:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><term>Multiple ways to engage</term> — text, diagrams, interactive tools,
        and examples let you choose what works best for you.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Multiple ways to represent ideas</term> — we use words, algebra,
        diagrams, and concrete examples so you see the same concept in different ways.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Multiple ways to show what you know</term> — exercises range from
        simple checks to complex problem-solving, so you can demonstrate understanding
        at your level.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Assessment as Learning (AAL)</term> — this isn't just about marks or grades.
        Every exercise helps you understand where you are, what you need to work on, and how you're
        growing. When you see hints, answers, and solutions, use them to learn from your thinking,
        not just to check if you're right.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-structure">
    <title>How This Book Is Organised</title>
    <p>
      Each of the five strands is a chapter. Within each chapter, sections focus on
      specific learning outcomes — the skills and knowledge you need to master. At the
      start of each section, you'll see the learning outcome clearly stated, and you can
      click it to understand what you're aiming for.
    </p>
    <p>
      Throughout the book, the <term>Unifying Strand</term> runs through everything.
      It's not a separate chapter — it's woven into every section. You'll be building
      blocks (understanding fundamentals), representing problems in different ways,
      making connections, solving problems, and communicating your mathematical thinking.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-assessment">
    <title>Assessment: How You're Evaluated</title>
    <p>
      Your Junior Cycle mathematics is assessed in three ways:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><term>School-based assessment (Year 2)</term> — a practical investigation
        task where you apply mathematics to a real problem.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>School-based assessment (Year 3)</term> — a statistics investigation
        where you collect data and analyse it using statistical methods.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Terminal examination (End of Year 3)</term> — a written exam covering
        all five strands and the Unifying Strand.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This book prepares you for all three. Every exercise helps you build the
      understanding and skills you need.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-learning-to-learn">
    <title>Learning to Learn</title>
    <p>
      One of the most important things you'll develop in Junior Cycle is not just mathematics
      knowledge — it's the ability to learn. This means:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><strong>Knowing how you learn best.</strong> Do you learn by doing? By visualising?
        By discussing? This book offers all three.</p></li>
      <li><p><strong>Asking good questions.</strong> "Why?" and "What if?" are more valuable
        than memorising rules.</p></li>
      <li><p><strong>Learning from mistakes.</strong> Errors are information, not failure.
        When you get something wrong, use it to understand better.</p></li>
      <li><p><strong>Reflecting on your progress.</strong> After each section, ask yourself:
        Can I do what the learning outcome says? Do I understand why, not just how?</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These skills — self-awareness, curiosity, persistence, reflection — will serve you
      far beyond mathematics.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-for-students">
    <title>For Students: How to Use This Book</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><strong>Start with the learning outcome.</strong> Each section begins with
          what you should be able to do by the end. Keep it in mind.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Work through examples and exercises.</strong> Don't just read them —
          actually solve them. The hints, answers, and solutions are there to help, not to
          skip ahead.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Use the diagrams.</strong> Mathematics is visual. Sketch things,
          draw on paper, use the interactive tools.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Make connections.</strong> The Unifying Strand outcomes remind you
          to link ideas within and between strands. When you see a connection, that's
          deep learning.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Ask yourself why.</strong> Don't memorise. Understand why a definition
          is written that way, why a theorem is true, why a method works.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Return to the intro.</strong> When you see a learning outcome referenced,
          click it. You'll jump back here to the big picture. Mathematics is not isolated
          facts — it's connected.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-for-parents">
    <title>For Parents and Guardians: How to Support Your Child</title>
    <p>
      Your child is learning Junior Cycle mathematics — a shift towards reasoning,
      problem-solving, and real-world thinking, not just calculations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><strong>Encourage struggle.</strong> It's okay if your child finds
          something hard. Struggling builds understanding. Ask, "Why does that work?"
          not "Did you get the right answer?"</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Celebrate connections.</strong> When your child links maths to
          something real — a recipe, a building, a sport — that's deep learning.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Support the investigations.</strong> The two school-based assessments
          are practical tasks. Help your child understand the problem and think through
          their approach, but let them do the mathematics.</p></li>
        <li><p><strong>This book is available online.</strong> Your child can access it
          anytime, anywhere — it's not confined to the classroom.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </section>

</preface>
